  "notes": [
    "Performance Schema is off (the MariaDB default), so metadata locks, such as a table held open while ALTER TABLE waits, are not visible. To see them, add performance_schema=ON and performance-schema-instrument='wait/lock/metadata/sql/mdl=ON' under [mysqld] in my.cnf and restart.",
    "An idle transaction's last statement needs Performance Schema with the events_statements_current consumer; MariaDB has both off by default.",
    "MariaDB lists only the row locks involved in a wait (information_schema.INNODB_LOCKS). Locks that block nobody are not visible through SQL; rows locked and lock structs in the transactions table count them. MariaDB also shows record-only and next-key locks both as X, and gap and insert-intention locks both as X,GAP.",
    "Rows a transaction inserted or changed are protected by implicit locks, which InnoDB lists only after another session conflicts with them. A transaction can hold more locks than the list shows.",
    "myflames never ends sessions. To end a blocker yourself: KILL QUERY n stops only its running statement, and the transaction keeps its row locks until it commits or rolls back. KILL n closes the connection and rolls back its open transaction, which can take a while after many changes.",
    "Each section comes from a separate query, so a lock can appear or disappear between sections."
  ],
